      "id": "grammar-indirect-object-pronoun-plus-a-clarifier",
      "kind": "grammar",
      "promptEs": "le/les + verbo … a + receptor explícito",
      "questionEn": "Why can Spanish use le or les with a verb and then still name the receiver with a phrase beginning with a?",
      "answerEn": "Use le or les with the verb, then use a phrase beginning with the word a to identify the receiver explicitly.",
      "noteEn": "Le is for one receiver and les for more than one. It normally appears before a conjugated verb, but it can attach to an unchanged verb form, as in darle. The later a phrase clarifies who or what the short marker refers to; Spanish often keeps both pieces even though natural English states the receiver only once. In the prompt, the slash means that le and les are alternatives, each plus sign means “followed by,” and the dots mean that other words may appear between the verb and the named receiver.",
      "example": {
        "es": "Le di agua al perro.",
        "en": "I gave the dog water."
      },
